WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:03.360
OK, so I was about to get on the rollercoaster

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:05.800
and then I chickened out...

00:00:05.800 --> 00:00:09.760
Chickened out?! Is that a verb?

00:00:09.760 --> 00:00:13.120
Ah, yes. It's a phrasal verb. I forgot

00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:17.800
students often freak out about them. Freak out...

00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:20.800
Is that another phrasal verbs? Yeah.

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:23.040
Please explain more.

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:25.120
OK! Here are some things 

00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:26.800
yiu should know about phrasal verbs.

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:33.400
Let's get into it. Phrasal verbs are verbs with
more than one word, like 'freak out',

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:35.920
'wake up' and 'put on'.

00:00:35.920 --> 00:00:37.880
They consist of a basic verb

00:00:37.880 --> 00:00:41.920
plus one - and sometimes two - extra words.

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:44.880
Phrasal verbs can be split into categories.

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:48.480
The first distinction is
between phrasal verbs which need

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:54.280
an object and phrasal verbs
which do not need an object.

00:00:54.280 --> 00:00:55.480
OK, woah,
woah, woah.

00:00:55.480 --> 00:00:56.600
Don't freak out.

00:00:56.600 --> 00:00:58.160
Let's look at some examples.

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:05.920
For verbs like pick up, put on, and turn on
you are doing the action to something.

00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:09.080
These phrasal verbs
require an object.

00:01:09.080 --> 00:01:12.320
You can't say 'I put on' on its own

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:18.760
without adding something. You put
on what? I put on my scarf.

00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:21.080
'My scarf' is the object.

00:01:21.080 --> 00:01:26.080
OK, and what about the other type? Verbs like 'stand up'

00:01:26.080 --> 00:01:27.360
and 'freak out'

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:31.640
do not require an object.
When I stand up,

00:01:31.640 --> 00:01:33.920
I stand up - and that's it.

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:36.960
I am not doing the action to anything.

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:38.800
We don't need an object.

00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:41.160
OK, and that's it?

00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:43.280
Er, no.

00:01:43.400 --> 00:01:44.600
Phrasal verbs that require

00:01:44.600 --> 00:01:46.880
an object can be split again

00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:50.800
into phrasal verbs that can be separated
by their object

00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:53.600
and phrasal verbs that cannot be separated

00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:55.000
by their object.

00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:57.360
For example, the verb 'put on'

00:01:57.360 --> 00:01:59.160
can be separated.

00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:05.040
You can say 'I put on my scarf'
or 'I put my scarf on'.

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:07.880
You can put the object in the middle of the verb or

00:02:07.880 --> 00:02:12.120
or at the end, and it means exactly the same thing.

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:14.600
However, the verb 'look after'

00:02:14.600 --> 00:02:16.760
can not be separated.

00:02:16.760 --> 00:02:19.960
You can say 'my cousin looks after my dog',

00:02:19.960 --> 00:02:22.840
but you cannot say 'my cousin looks

00:02:22.840 --> 00:02:26.560
my dog after'. It sounds strange
and very wrong.

00:02:26.560 --> 00:02:31.520
Okay. I'm freaking out. How am I supposed to learn all of this?!

00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:36.360
Don't freak out! Just come back to BBC Learning English. Where we'll

00:02:36.360 --> 00:02:39.360
teach you more phrasal verbs and how to use them.

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:41.080
See you next time.

